Let the Echo's action button skip tracks.

The daemon already holds the controller role, so it can send Music
Assistant the same play, pause, next and previous a Sendspin remote
sends - it just had no way to be asked. This gives it a root-only
datagram socket, /run/sendspin-control: one command word per datagram
(play, pause, stop, next, previous, or toggle, which picks play or
pause from the playback state the server last reported). biscuit-va-leds
writes to it when a button gesture is bound to a media action.

Previous always goes back a track. Music Assistant restarts the current
track instead once it is 5 s in - too short to hit with a button - so
the daemon follows the track position from the server's metadata and,
past that point, sends previous twice: the first restarts the track and
zeroes its elapsed time at once, the second steps back, and the server's
one-second debounce plays only the result.
A new track that arrives without fresh progress drops the old position
rather than extrapolate it, and a reconnect forgets the last session's
position and playback state.

A socket that fails to open only logs: the speaker keeps playing.

